> Hi! If I want to say "I have a car. The car is white.":
> (I'm interested only in differences between {le} and {lo}.)
> 1. with {le}: mi ponse le karce .i le karce cu blabi
> 2. with {lo}: mi ponse lo karce .i lo karce cu blabi
> I think the first choice is better. Am I right?
>

I would still use the second choice, because to me, the most important
feature of {le} is not the specificity (Russian gets by without a
distinction between specific and non-specific just fine), but the
non-veridicality (i.e. that {lo broda cu broda} is tautological but that
{le broda cu broda} is not).

Better than the second choice, I'd probably use
1. {mi ponse lo karce .i ky blabi} or
2. {mi ponse lo karce noi blabi} or even
3. {lo karce ge se ponse mi gi blabi}.


In both the sentences you gave, the second {karce} isn't necessarily, but
is probably, the same as the first {karce}; in the ones I gave, it's clear
that I'm referring to the same car (well, (1.) is slightly ambiguous, but
less so than yours

Both your statements are comparable to "=F5 =CD=C5=CE=D1 =CD=C1=DB=C9=CE=C1=
. =ED=C1=DB=C9=CE=C1 =CB=D2=C1=D3=CE=C1=D1."
